ollaborators so yeah many  people to thank  cesare tonelli and clark barrett are the  project leads for the uh  the team so um  to give some setting to the kind of  formalisms i'll be using in this talk so  uh  we're interested in synthesis  conjectures of this form so there exists  some function f  for which some property p holds for all  x  and uh more specifically we're  interested in predicates where  [Music]  the background theory is something like  linear arithmetic or any of the other  theories supported by smt solvers  so this theory component kind of often  motivates you to use  an smt solver  additional additionally in in addition  to the spec here  we have a syntax and so this is the  classic  syntax guided synthesis problem  which rajiv and others popularized  recently and uh  i'd like to just go over this uh kind of  standard picture of  a counterexample guided inductive  synthesis so given a syntax and a  spec you have a solution enumerator and  a solution verifier  the enumerator uh gives uh candidate  solutions  for f and uh  it enumerates them until a the solution  verifier is able to find  that there is uh a given f  has no counter examples with respect to  the spec  and the body of these functions here are  given by the syntax  so it's a pretty common picture here so  in general the smt solver or in  traditional settings has been used as  the solution  verifier but um  in our cab 15 paper we sort of  considered a different  architecture where the solution  enumeration and verification were  kind of tightly wound together in the  smt solver  in particular in cbc4 and what this  allows is  certain synthesis algorithms that  leverage  the internal state of the s t solver  become possible  and just in general for performance  reasons  uh you often really would like a tight  integration between these two components  so in this talk i'm actually going to  give you three different flavors of  synthesis in uh cbc4 how we  how we um solve these uh synthesis  conjectures  um i'll briefly mention some internal  applications of sigis that we have for  actually making  fork better and just briefly mention  some future work  so uh given this kind of high level  picture we have this  synthesis conjecture we want cbc4 to  somehow magically solve it and give us  the solution  well as i mentioned there's actually  three different independent solvers  within  cbc4 and  the first is counter example guided  quantifier instantiation this is an idea  that we  brought up in cav 2015 and we've been  working on extensions of it  in the past few years there's also what  we call the smart enumerative solver and  the fast enumerative solver and  again you know i'm just giving some  references here to some of the papers  where we describe these  techniques and uh the best  approach to apply here depends uh on the  synthesis conjecture i'll get into you  know what kind of conjectures are given  to which one of these solvers  so just going over the three techniques  now so the first one is what we call  counter example guided  quantifier instantiation and the  intuition here  is that some synthesis conjectures are  essentially first order  um and in particular this uh is the  classic max example saying that f  x y is the maximum of x and y  and what you can do with a conjecture  like this is what's known as  anti-scolization where the outermost  function  f is pushed inwards to become a first  order variable  z and then it's in a first order form  and we know how to solve these in  in smt solvers and the techniques that  we use to solve them  are under the uh umbrella of counter  example guided quantifier instantiation  so i'm not going to get into the details  of this technique but uh  the the high level ideas is mentioned  here is that  essentially there's classic quantifier  elimination procedures  that uh typically you can phrase as  instantiation-based procedures for  first order quantified formulas with one  alternation  and from this a synthesis procedure for  single and multiplication  properties follows fairly trivially  however there's quite a few caveats here  so  first of all as i mentioned the  specification must be single invocation  so the functions to synthesize in your  problem  are applied to the list of universal  variables so this max  example works you can have multiple  functions as long as they're applied to  the same arguments  but more complicated conjectures like  saying that a function is commutative  this technique is not applicable  caveat is if syntax restrictions are  present then  this method may violate them and what we  do in practice is  a heuristic fitting of the solution that  we get from  counterexample guided instantiation to  the grammar  and third a term selection strategy must  be known for the theory so  we don't magically get a synthesis  procedure for for new theories we  actually have to do the  theory theory-specific work of  determining uh how to  to uh devise this selection strategy  so for linear arithmetic small finite  domains bit vectors  these things we know how to deal with  but more  complex theories like strings and  nonlinear arithmetic we don't  okay so given all those caveats um  a more generic way of solving synthesis  conjectures in smt solvers  we use enumerative approaches and  the first of these is what i call smart  enumerative psygis  and so um in smart and enumerative  cyclists again we have this picture of  having a conjecture and a set of syntax  restrictions but what we do is it  alternatively instead of looking at as  this as syntax restrictions we look at  the grammar as an inductive data type  and the inductive data type inductive  data types is a  theory that actually smt solvers support  so then what we do with this input  conjecture is we turn this  higher order quantification on f  we we turn it into first order  quantification on this data type which  i'm calling f  int and this is done via a deep  embedding  where it also