rding  terms that are not unique up to theory  rewriting  and the other pro is that fast  enumeration is  indeed very fast now if you actually  compare a lightweight data structure for  enumerating  terms you get roughly 100 times the  throughput  with respect to smart enumeration  so that means it's considering roughly  100 times more candidates per second  and the reason for that is just you know  using a sat solver to to generate  candidate solutions versus  using custom iterators it's  you know a heavy hammer versus a light  hammer  uh the main con though when using uh  fast enumeration is you cannot  easily generalize syntactic constraints  and some of these more advanced  techniques for pruning the search space  are not easily applicable  and the reason for that is essentially  sometimes you need a constraint solver  to to really solve you know  navigating a search space  but uh the the summary is in fact fast  enumeration  is usually better for most of the  um sigis comp benchmarks however  when you're really dealing with  difficult problems you should use  a smart enumeration generally so in  particular for invariant synthesis  problems we almost always use smart  if you really wanted to get into the  guts of you know what what  keeps me up at night is you know this is  uh  some profiling information about how  to optimize uh fast enumerative sigis  and um just this is kind of giving the  lifetime of a term  through um  through fast enumeration so considering  a search space of about 10 million terms  we generally construct about 100 000 of  them and  look at the rewriter of those we  consider only about sixty thousand  um to evaluate on examples  uh fifteen thousand we may check against  the conjecture and so on  and uh the takeaway here is actually  that evaluating terms on concrete  examples is surprisingly the bottleneck  um when it comes to when you're really  getting down into  um what is the bottleneck for a new root  of sigis so in fact about 70  of 